      ],
      "key": "installed_release_basis",
      "label": "Installed Release Basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A read-only deterministic acceptance object binding current Product Definition identity/bytes/current basis to a resolved immutable target cut, annotated tag, commit, tree and manifest digest. SHA-256 is an operator-presentable acceptance token whose possession alone grants no authority. A Fleet Adoption Plan binds an authorized fleet root and complete ordered per-definition plan digests.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 524,
          "quote": "An **Adoption Plan** is the read-only, deterministic acceptance object binding\none current Product Definition's identity and bytes to its current basis and\none resolved immutable target's cut, annotated tag object, commit, tree, and\ninstalled-manifest digest. Its SHA-256 is the operator-presentable acceptance\ntoken; possession alone does not grant authority, while a mismatch proves that\nthe presented subject is no longer the subject being mutated. A **Fleet\nAdoption Plan** binds an authorized fleet root and the complete ordered set of\nper-definition plan digests under the same law.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#Spec-Driven%20Homeostatic%20Methodology%2FSTDO%20Product%20Definition%20Overlay%20And%20Layout%20Independence%2FShared%20Installed%20Release%20Basis%20And%20Toolchain%20Manager",
          "start_line": 517
        }
      ],
      "key": "adoption_plan",
      "label": "Adoption Plan and Fleet Adoption Plan"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A bounded executable realization that installs immutable cuts once in a shared versioned store, resolves logical release URIs, inventories/verifies complete distributions, synchronizes selected exact bases, presents digest-bound adoption plans and adopts only on explicit acceptance, manages marker-bounded bootstrap blocks, and operates over explicitly discovered fleets.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 543,
          "quote": "The **STDO Toolchain Manager** is the bounded executable realization that:\n\n- installs each immutable cut once in a shared, versioned release store;\n- resolves logical `stdo://releases/...` URIs to verified installed bytes;\n- inventories and verifies complete installed distributions;\n- synchronizes the exact basis already selected by a Product Definition;\n- emits an exact digest-bound adoption plan from a mutable version-line\n  selector and performs adoption only when that plan is explicitly accepted;\n- manages the stable marker-bounded bootstrap in declared agent files; and\n- applies those operations to an explicitly discovered fleet of Product\n  Definitions.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#Spec-Driven%20Homeostatic%20Methodology%2FSTDO%20Product%20Definition%20Overlay%20And%20Layout%20Independence%2FShared%20Installed%20Release%20Basis%20And%20Toolchain%20Manager",
          "start_line": 533
        }
      ],
      "key": "stdo_toolchain_manager",
      "label": "STDO Toolchain Manager"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An invocation-selected complete update plan binds one Product Definition and all companions in one exact upstream release-matched cohort to owner-selected consumer bindings. It adds physical destinations, preimages, cohort bytes, companion tags/commits/subtrees/inventories, replacement dependency locators, native routes and derived-source observations to its canonical acceptance object. The plan/result is operation evidence, not a persistent dependency declaration, registry or lock.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 640,
          "quote": "A **Complete Consumer Update Plan** binds one explicitly selected Product\nDefinition and the complete companion population of one exact upstream\nrelease-matched cohort to the consumer bindings selected by the invocation's\nowner. The Product Definition continues to own composition; the upstream\ncohort and each Product's release record own release and member identity.\nAn invocation selection and its derived plan/result are operation evidence,\nnot another persistent dependency declaration, registry or lock. The caller\nmust select every native route and derived context for the claimed update\nscope; directory proximity or an empty discovery result cannot supply that\nselection. An empty derived-context population means the owner selected none,\nnot that the manager established the absence of semantic context.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#Spec-Driven%20Homeostatic%20Methodology%2FSTDO%20Product%20Definition%20Overlay%20And%20Layout%20Independence%2FShared%20Installed%20Release%20Basis%20And%20Toolchain%20Manager%2FExplicit%20Complete%20Consumer%20Update",
          "start_line": 630
        }
      ],
      "key": "complete_consumer_update",
      "label": "Complete Consumer Update Plan and operation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A term is a lexical label in an occurrence. A bounded context is a declared stable semantic scope with owner, selected basis and jointly interpretable vocabulary/concepts/relations. A concept reference resolves exact source-owned meaning without minting authority. The semantic address is (term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope). SPEC_METHOD.md owns this law in urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 703,
          "quote": "A **term** is a lexical label used in an occurrence, not a context-free concept\nidentity. A **bounded context** is an explicitly identified semantic scope in\nwhich a governed vocabulary, concept set, and relation set are jointly\ninterpretable under named authority. A **concept reference** is an exact,\nresolvable reference to a meaning owned by an authority under a selected basis;\nthe reference locates that meaning but does not mint it or its authority.\n\nA bounded context exists only when an owning authority declares its stable\nidentity, owner, selected basis, and governed semantic scope.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#Spec-Driven%20Homeostatic%20Methodology%2FSTDO%20Product%20Definition%20Overlay%20And%20Layout%20Independence%2FBounded-Context%20Semantic%20Resolution",
          "start_line": 692
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_resolution",
      "label": "Bounded-context semantic resolution"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Explicit owner-authorized cross-context relations distinguish Semantic Import (unchanged meaning in target scope), Semantic Translation (directional meaning change), Semantic Equivalence (authority-established equality), and Semantic Disambiguation (one meaning selected for one scope without equating competitors). Specialization is a directional translation preserving declared source meaning while narrowing admissible instances or adding target constraints.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 780,
          "quote": "A **Semantic Import** adopts the cited meaning unchanged for its declared target\nscope. A **Semantic Translation** declares how meaning changes in one direction.\n**Semantic Equivalence** requires the governing authorities to establish the\nclaimed equality; neither identical spelling nor two opposing translations\nproves it automatically. A **Semantic Disambiguation** selects one meaning for\none scope and does not make competing concepts equal. No relation widens its\nsource or target authority.\n\nA specialization is a directional translation whose target preserves the\ndeclared source meaning while narrowing its admissible instances or adding\ntarget-context constraints.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/SPEC_METHOD.md#Spec-Driven%20Homeostatic%20Methodology%2FSTDO%20Product%20Definition%20Overlay%20And%20Layout%20Independence%2FBounded-Context%20Semantic%20Resolution",
          "start_line": 768
        }
      ],
      "key": "cross_context_semantic_relation",
      "label": "Cross-context semantic relations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Local axioms, overrides and disambiguations bind an existing lawful owner, decision carrier, applicable target or ambiguity and exact scope. The overlay exposes the relation but neither restates owned meanings nor creates the decision.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 792,
          "quote": "A local axiom, override, or disambiguation does not gain authority from its file\ncategory or from being listed in the overlay. Every local entry names:\n\n- the URI carrying the local decision;\n- the existing Product, requirement, accepted-design, or other lawful authority\n  that owns it;\n- the clause or ambiguity it overrides or resolves where applicable; and\n- the exact scope to which it applies.",
