      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 20,
          "quote": "Candidate preservation, acceptance,\npublication and consumer adoption remain distinct decisions. Its version/ref\nexamples illustrate the declared release identities; each actual cut requires\nits own authorised exact binding.\nResult bindings below preserve core legal performance pairs, diagnostic\nprecedence, absent coverage and actual effects. They describe the existing\nchecks and decisions without adding an evaluation stage or evidence carrier.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/RELEASE_METHOD.md#Release%20Method",
          "start_line": 14
        },
        {
          "end_line": 36,
          "quote": "The following arguments apply the adopted\n[F1–F6 commitments](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundational-commitments) to the instance\npremises stated in each row. The pillars contribute identity, preserved\nrelations, observable execution and justified decisions to distinct release\nquestions. The named consumers own the release policies; none follows from\nfinite resources alone.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/RELEASE_METHOD.md#Release%20Method%2FRelease-Publication%20Context",
          "start_line": 31
        }
      ],
      "key": "release_authority_and_premise_boundary",
      "statement": "Acceptance claims need one reacquirable subject and evidence for declared predicates. Exact-cut qualification/acceptance use shared result/conjunction; the transformation library supplies Evaluate/Conjoin/Dispose responsibilities. Immutable cuts, Git ref profiles and monotonic selectors are additional release choices, not consequences of finite capacity or successful construction. Preserve distinct candidate-preservation, qualification, acceptance, publication and adoption predicates/decisions, legal performance pairs, precedence, absent coverage and effects without adding stages/carriers. Each actual cut needs its own authorized binding. Context arguments distinguish Product membership from shared carrier; preserve historical reference denotation; require remote observation after uncertain dispatch; match evidence boundaries to claims and reserve human acceptance; separate mutable discovery from adopted basis and accepted plans; require declared cohort joins/source congruence without transferring owners; and assess predecessor meaning rather than names/code shape. Product authority owns subjects, namespaces, claim scopes, cohort selection, removal and accepted replacements; Release owns its named ref/publication/selector/cohort policies; Spec owns Product taxonomy/bindings. None of these arguments grants operation authority.",
      "uncertainty": "Foundational commitments, transformation contracts and some linked consumers are outside the supplied source."
    },
    {
